window.CHDATA=window.CHDATA||{};window.CHDATA[20]={"num": 20, "title": "Tick-Borne Infections", "keyPoints": "<ul>\n<li>Ticks transmit <strong>bacteria</strong> (<em>Borrelia</em>, <em>Rickettsia</em>, <em>Anaplasma</em>, <em>Ehrlichia</em>, <em>Francisella</em>), <strong>protozoa</strong> (<em>Babesia</em>) and <strong>viruses</strong> (tick-borne encephalitis virus, Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever virus). One bite can transmit <strong>more than one pathogen</strong> (co-infection).</li>\n<li><strong>Lyme borreliosis</strong> is the most common tick-borne infection in Europe and North America. <strong>Erythema migrans (EM)</strong> is a <strong>clinical diagnosis</strong>: do <strong>not</strong> order serology for a typical EM.</li>\n<li><strong>Lyme serology</strong> is a <strong>two-tier</strong> test (screening immunoassay, then immunoblot or second immunoassay), only for patients with compatible <strong>extracutaneous</strong> disease. <strong>Neuroborreliosis</strong> is confirmed by <strong>CSF pleocytosis + intrathecal anti-<em>Borrelia</em> antibodies</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Doxycycline is the key drug</strong>: first-line for most Lyme manifestations, for <strong>all rickettsioses</strong> and for <strong>anaplasmosis/ehrlichiosis</strong>, <strong>at any age</strong>. Start it <strong>empirically</strong> when a spotted fever is suspected; never wait for serology.</li>\n<li><strong>Mediterranean spotted fever</strong> (<em>Rickettsia conorii</em>, brown dog tick) is endemic in <strong>southern Italy and the islands</strong>: <strong>fever + maculopapular rash including palms and soles + tache noire</strong> (eschar).</li>\n<li><strong>Babesiosis</strong> looks like malaria on the smear (ring forms, <strong>\"Maltese cross\"</strong> tetrads) and is severe in <strong>asplenic</strong> and immunocompromised patients; treat with <strong>atovaquone + azithromycin</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Tick-borne encephalitis (TBE)</strong> is typically <strong>biphasic</strong>; it has <strong>no specific treatment</strong> but is preventable with an <strong>inactivated vaccine</strong>. <strong>Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever (CCHF)</strong> is a <strong>viral haemorrhagic fever</strong> with <strong>nosocomial transmission</strong>, now autochthonous in <strong>Spain</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Prevention</strong> rests on <strong>avoiding bites</strong>, <strong>repellents</strong>, daily <strong>tick checks</strong> and <strong>prompt removal</strong> with fine-tipped tweezers.</li>\n</ul>", "redFlags": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Erythema migrans = diagnosis.</strong> Do not order serology; treat (doxycycline 10 days).</li>\n<li><strong>No tick bite history does not exclude</strong> a tick-borne infection.</li>\n<li><strong>Fever + rash on palms and soles + black eschar</strong> in summer in southern Italy = <strong>Mediterranean spotted fever</strong> → <strong>doxycycline now</strong>, serology later.</li>\n<li><strong>Doxycycline is safe for children</strong> in short courses; withholding it in <strong>RMSF or MSF</strong> can be <strong>fatal</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fluoroquinolones worsen MSF</strong>; <strong>β-lactams do not work</strong> against rickettsiae, <em>Anaplasma</em>, <em>Ehrlichia</em> or <em>Francisella</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fever + leukopenia + thrombocytopenia + high transaminases</strong> after a tick bite: think <strong>anaplasmosis/ehrlichiosis</strong> (or early TBE).</li>\n<li><strong>Positive Lyme serology ≠ active disease</strong>: antibodies persist for years and are common in exposed people; <strong>an isolated IgM after 4 weeks of illness is probably false positive</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Neuroborreliosis</strong> needs <strong>CSF</strong>: pleocytosis + intrathecal antibodies. <strong>Bilateral facial palsy</strong> is a classic clue.</li>\n<li><strong>Lyme carditis:</strong> PR ≥300 ms → admit, monitor, temporary pacing if needed; <strong>no permanent pacemaker</strong> in the acute phase.</li>\n<li><strong>PTLDS:</strong> <strong>no prolonged antibiotic therapy</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>\"Malaria\" smear without travel to the tropics</strong>, in an <strong>asplenic</strong> patient → <strong>babesiosis</strong>; no pigment, <strong>Maltese cross</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>TBE: tick removal does not prevent it</strong> (virus in tick saliva); also transmitted by <strong>raw milk</strong>; serology <strong>cross-reacts with flavivirus vaccines</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>CCHF: fever + bleeding</strong> after tick bite or <strong>contact with livestock blood</strong> → <strong>isolate, notify, protect staff</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Tularemia: warn the lab</strong> before sending cultures; <strong>gentamicin</strong> for severe cases, <strong>ciprofloxacin</strong> for mild.</li>\n<li><strong>Relapsing fever:</strong> draw the <strong>smear during fever</strong>; expect a <strong>Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction</strong> after the first dose.</li>\n</ul>", "intro": "", "sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Microbiology", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "1.1", "title": "Ticks as vectors", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Ticks are <strong>arachnids</strong> (not insects). Their life cycle has <strong>three stages</strong> (<strong>larva → nymph → adult</strong>), and each stage takes <strong>one blood meal</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2,28]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hard ticks (Ixodidae)</strong> stay attached and feed for <strong>several days</strong>; <strong>soft ticks (Argasidae)</strong> feed for <strong>minutes</strong>, usually at <strong>night</strong>, so patients often <strong>do not notice the bite</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[17,28]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Nymphs</strong> (1–2 mm) transmit most Lyme borreliosis: they are active in <strong>late spring and summer</strong> and are easily missed<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Time to transmission</strong> differs between pathogens and explains why early tick removal protects against some infections but not others<sup class=\"cite\">[1,6,9]</sup>:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pathogen</th>\n<th>Where it lives in the tick</th>\n<th>Attachment time needed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em> s.l.</td>\n<td><strong>Midgut</strong>; must migrate to salivary glands</td>\n<td>Risk is <strong>low in the first 24 h</strong> and rises after <strong>36–48 h</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Rickettsia rickettsii</em></td>\n<td>Salivary glands; needs \"reactivation\" by the blood meal</td>\n<td><strong>Hours</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>TBE virus</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Salivary glands</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Minutes</strong>: early removal does <strong>not</strong> prevent TBE<sup class=\"cite\">[9]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tick</th>\n<th>Main pathogens</th>\n<th>Distribution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>Ixodes ricinus</em></strong> (castor bean tick)</td>\n<td><em>B. burgdorferi</em> s.l., TBE virus, <em>A. phagocytophilum</em>, <em>B. divergens</em>, <em>B. miyamotoi</em>, some rickettsiae</td>\n<td><strong>Europe</strong>, including northern and central Italy<sup class=\"cite\">[2,9]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>Ixodes scapularis</em> / <em>I. pacificus</em></strong> (blacklegged ticks)</td>\n<td><em>B. burgdorferi</em> sensu stricto, <em>A. phagocytophilum</em>, <em>B. microti</em>, Powassan virus, <em>B. miyamotoi</em></td>\n<td><strong>North America</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,7]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>Rhipicephalus sanguineus</em></strong> (brown dog tick)</td>\n<td><strong><em>R. conorii</em></strong> (Mediterranean spotted fever)</td>\n<td><strong>Mediterranean</strong>, around dogs and houses<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>Dermacentor</em></strong> spp.</td>\n<td><em>R. rickettsii</em> (USA), <em>R. slovaca</em> (Europe), <em>F. tularensis</em></td>\n<td>Americas, Europe<sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>Amblyomma</em></strong> spp.</td>\n<td><em>E. chaffeensis</em> and <em>R. rickettsii</em> (Americas), <strong><em>R. africae</em></strong> (sub-Saharan Africa)</td>\n<td>Americas, Africa<sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>Hyalomma</em></strong> spp. (especially <em>H. marginatum</em>)</td>\n<td><strong>CCHF virus</strong></td>\n<td>Africa, Balkans, Turkey, Middle East, Asia, <strong>Spain</strong>; carried by migratory birds<sup class=\"cite\">[12,13]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>Ornithodoros</em></strong> spp. (soft ticks)</td>\n<td><strong>Tick-borne relapsing fever</strong> <em>Borrelia</em></td>\n<td>Africa, Iberian Peninsula, Middle East, Central Asia, western USA<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "1.2", "title": "Overview of the pathogens", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Disease</th>\n<th>Agent</th>\n<th>Type of organism</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lyme borreliosis</strong></td>\n<td><strong><em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em> sensu lato</strong> (<em>B. afzelii</em>, <em>B. garinii</em>, <em>B. burgdorferi</em> sensu stricto, <em>B. bavariensis</em>, <em>B. mayonii</em>)</td>\n<td><strong>Spirochete</strong>; microaerophilic, very slow growing; also proposed as genus <em>Borreliella</em><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Spotted fever rickettsioses</strong></td>\n<td><em>Rickettsia conorii</em>, <em>R. rickettsii</em>, <em>R. africae</em>, <em>R. slovaca</em> and others</td>\n<td><strong>Obligate intracellular</strong> Gram-negative bacteria that infect <strong>endothelial cells</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Human granulocytic anaplasmosis (HGA)</strong></td>\n<td><strong><em>Anaplasma phagocytophilum</em></strong></td>\n<td>Obligate intracellular bacterium of <strong>neutrophils</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6,19]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Human monocytic ehrlichiosis (HME)</strong></td>\n<td><strong><em>Ehrlichia chaffeensis</em></strong></td>\n<td>Obligate intracellular bacterium of <strong>monocytes/macrophages</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tularemia</strong></td>\n<td><strong><em>Francisella tularensis</em></strong> subsp. <strong><em>tularensis</em></strong> (type A, North America, more virulent) and subsp. <strong><em>holarctica</em></strong> (type B, Europe and Asia)</td>\n<td>Small, facultatively intracellular <strong>Gram-negative coccobacillus</strong>; <strong>highly infectious</strong> (10–50 organisms)<sup class=\"cite\">[16,23]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Babesiosis</strong></td>\n<td><strong><em>Babesia microti</em></strong> (USA), <strong><em>B. divergens</em></strong> and <em>B. venatorum</em> (Europe)</td>\n<td><strong>Intraerythrocytic protozoa</strong> (Apicomplexa, like <em>Plasmodium</em>)<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tick-borne relapsing fever (TBRF)</strong></td>\n<td><em>B. hispanica</em>, <em>B. duttonii</em>, <em>B. crocidurae</em>, <em>B. persica</em>, <em>B. hermsii</em>; <strong><em>B. miyamotoi</em></strong> (transmitted by <em>Ixodes</em>)</td>\n<td><strong>Spirochetes</strong> with <strong>antigenic variation</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tick-borne encephalitis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>TBE virus</strong>: European, Siberian and Far Eastern subtypes</td>\n<td><strong>Flavivirus</strong> (enveloped, single-stranded positive-sense RNA)<sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CCHF</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CCHF virus</strong> (<em>Orthonairovirus</em>)</td>\n<td><strong>Nairovirus</strong> (bunyavirus group), enveloped, <strong>segmented</strong> negative-sense RNA<sup class=\"cite\">[12,13]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><em>B. burgdorferi</em> s.l. genospecies and clinical tropism</strong> (high yield)<sup class=\"cite\">[2,3]</sup>:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Genospecies</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>Typical manifestations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>B. afzelii</em></strong></td>\n<td>Europe (most common)</td>\n<td><strong>Skin</strong>: EM, <strong>borrelial lymphocytoma</strong>, <strong>acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans (ACA)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>B. garinii</em></strong> (and <em>B. bavariensis</em>)</td>\n<td>Europe</td>\n<td><strong>Neuroborreliosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>B. burgdorferi</em> sensu stricto</strong></td>\n<td><strong>North America</strong> (only pathogenic species, with <em>B. mayonii</em>); also Europe</td>\n<td><strong>Arthritis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Rickettsia</em>, <em>Anaplasma</em>, <em>Ehrlichia</em> and <em>Borrelia</em> are <strong>not seen on Gram stain</strong> and are <strong>not grown in routine cultures</strong>, so diagnosis relies on <strong>PCR, serology and smears</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "2", "title": "Epidemiology", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "2.1", "title": "Lyme borreliosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>The most common vector-borne disease in the temperate Northern Hemisphere</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>USA:</strong> an estimated <strong>~476,000 patients</strong> are diagnosed and treated each year (2010–2018), concentrated in the <strong>Northeast and upper Midwest</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[22]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Europe:</strong> more than <strong>85,000 cases per year</strong> were reported in older estimates, a clear underestimate because reporting is incomplete; incidence is highest in <strong>central Europe and Scandinavia</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Italy:</strong> low incidence overall; most cases come from <strong>north-eastern regions</strong> (Friuli Venezia Giulia, Veneto, Trentino-Alto Adige)<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Seasonality:</strong> peak <strong>June–August</strong>, following nymph activity. At-risk people: <strong>hikers, forest workers, hunters</strong>, people living near woodland<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "2.2", "title": "Rickettsioses", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Mediterranean spotted fever (MSF; \"boutonneuse fever\")</strong>, caused by <em>R. conorii</em>, is endemic around the <strong>Mediterranean basin</strong>. <strong>Italy</strong> is one of the most affected European countries, especially <strong>Sicily, Sardinia, Calabria</strong> and other southern and central regions; most cases occur in <strong>summer</strong> (July–September), often in people with <strong>dog contact</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Other European rickettsioses<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>: <strong><em>R. slovaca</em></strong> (scalp eschar with neck lymphadenopathy, called <strong>TIBOLA/SENLAT</strong>, transmitted by <em>Dermacentor</em> in cold months), <strong><em>R. sibirica mongolitimonae</em></strong> (<strong>lymphangitis-associated rickettsiosis</strong>), <em>R. massiliae</em>, <em>R. helvetica</em>, <em>R. monacensis</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>African tick bite fever</strong> (<em>R. africae</em>) is the most common rickettsiosis in <strong>travellers</strong> returning from <strong>sub-Saharan Africa</strong> (safari, game parks)<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rocky Mountain spotted fever (RMSF)</strong> (<em>R. rickettsii</em>) occurs in the <strong>Americas</strong>; it is the <strong>most lethal</strong> rickettsiosis<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Not tick-borne (do not confuse):</strong> <strong>scrub typhus</strong> (<em>Orientia tsutsugamushi</em>, mites), <strong>epidemic typhus</strong> (<em>R. prowazekii</em>, body lice), <strong>murine typhus</strong> (<em>R. typhi</em>, fleas)<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "2.3", "title": "Anaplasmosis and ehrlichiosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>HGA</strong> is reported in the USA (same vector and areas as Lyme) and, less often, in <strong>Europe</strong>, where most infections are mild or asymptomatic<sup class=\"cite\">[19]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HME</strong> occurs mainly in the <strong>south-central and south-eastern USA</strong>, transmitted by the <strong>lone star tick</strong> (<em>Amblyomma americanum</em>)<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "2.4", "title": "Tularemia", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Found across the <strong>Northern Hemisphere</strong>: in Europe it is most frequent in <strong>Scandinavia, Finland and central-eastern Europe</strong>; Turkey and the Balkans have <strong>water-borne oropharyngeal</strong> outbreaks<sup class=\"cite\">[16,23]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reservoirs:</strong> <strong>hares, rabbits, rodents</strong> (voles, lemmings). <strong>Routes:</strong> tick or (in Scandinavia) <strong>mosquito bites</strong>, <strong>handling infected animals</strong> (hunters, butchers), <strong>contaminated water or food</strong>, <strong>inhaled aerosols</strong> (farming, haymaking, lawn mowing)<sup class=\"cite\">[16,23]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>No person-to-person transmission</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[23]</sup>. <em>F. tularensis</em> is a <strong>potential bioterrorism agent</strong> (CDC category A) because of its infectivity by aerosol<sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "2.5", "title": "Babesiosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong><em>B. microti</em></strong> is endemic in the <strong>north-eastern and upper Midwest USA</strong>; it is also transmitted by <strong>blood transfusion</strong> and, rarely, transplacentally<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li>In <strong>Europe</strong>, cases are rare and are classically caused by <strong><em>B. divergens</em></strong> (cattle parasite) in <strong>splenectomised</strong> patients, where the disease can be <strong>fulminant</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "2.6", "title": "Tick-borne relapsing fever", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Endemic</strong> in parts of <strong>Africa</strong> (<em>B. duttonii</em> in East Africa; <em>B. crocidurae</em> in West Africa, where it is a major cause of fever), the <strong>Iberian Peninsula and North Africa</strong> (<em>B. hispanica</em>), the <strong>Middle East and Central Asia</strong> (<em>B. persica</em>) and the <strong>western USA</strong> (<em>B. hermsii</em>, rustic <strong>cabins</strong> with rodent nests)<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong><em>B. miyamotoi</em></strong>, transmitted by the same <em>Ixodes</em> ticks as Lyme disease, is found in <strong>Europe, North America and Asia</strong> (\"hard tick relapsing fever\")<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Louse-borne relapsing fever</strong> (<em>B. recurrentis</em>, body lice) is a different disease; it has been seen in <strong>migrants from the Horn of Africa</strong> arriving in Europe<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "2.7", "title": "Tick-borne encephalitis", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Endemic in a <strong>belt from central and northern Europe to Siberia and Far East Asia</strong> (Japan, China)<sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>EU/EEA (ECDC, 2022):</strong> <strong>3,650 cases</strong> reported by 20 countries (<strong>3,516 confirmed</strong>), notification rate <strong>0.81 per 100,000</strong> (14% higher than 2021); <strong>90%</strong> of cases occurred <strong>June–November</strong>, more in <strong>men</strong> (ratio 1.5:1) and in the <strong>45–64 age group</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[20]</sup>.</li>\n<li>The disease is <strong>spreading to new areas</strong> and to <strong>higher altitudes</strong>, probably helped by climate change<sup class=\"cite\">[9,20]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Italy:</strong> endemic foci are in the <strong>north-east</strong> (Friuli Venezia Giulia, Veneto, Trentino-Alto Adige)<sup class=\"cite\">[20]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Alimentary transmission:</strong> TBE can also be acquired by drinking <strong>unpasteurised milk or dairy products</strong> from infected <strong>goats, sheep or cows</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "2.8", "title": "Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever", "html": "<ul>\n<li>The <strong>most widespread tick-borne viral disease</strong> of humans: <strong>Africa, the Balkans, Turkey, the Middle East, Russia and Central/South Asia</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[12,13]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spain</strong> reported its <strong>first autochthonous cases in 2016</strong> and has had sporadic cases since<sup class=\"cite\">[24]</sup>. <em>Hyalomma</em> ticks carried by <strong>migratory birds</strong> have been found in <strong>Italy</strong> and other countries without human cases<sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Routes</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[12,13,25]</sup>:</li>\n<li><strong>Tick bite</strong> or crushing ticks with bare hands.</li>\n<li>Contact with <strong>blood or tissues of viraemic livestock</strong> (animals are infected but <strong>asymptomatic</strong>): <strong>farmers, slaughterhouse workers, veterinarians</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Human-to-human</strong>: contact with <strong>blood and body fluids</strong> of patients, including <strong>nosocomial outbreaks</strong> among <strong>healthcare workers</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Case fatality: about 10–40%</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[25]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "3", "title": "Pathogenesis", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Lyme borreliosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li>During the blood meal, <em>B. burgdorferi</em> switches its main surface protein from <strong>OspA</strong> (needed in the tick midgut) to <strong>OspC</strong> (needed to infect the mammal)<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>. <strong>Tick saliva</strong> suppresses local immunity<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li>The spirochetes multiply in the skin and <strong>spread centrifugally</strong> (the expanding <strong>EM</strong>), then disseminate through <strong>blood and lymph</strong> to <strong>other skin sites, nervous system, heart and joints</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><em>Borrelia</em> produces <strong>no toxins</strong>; symptoms are due to the <strong>host inflammatory response</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Antibiotic-refractory (post-infectious) Lyme arthritis</strong> is an <strong>immune-mediated</strong> synovitis that persists after the spirochetes are killed; it is associated with certain <strong>HLA-DR alleles</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Spotted fever rickettsioses", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Rickettsiae enter at the bite site (<strong>eschar = local necrosis</strong>), then infect <strong>vascular endothelial cells</strong> of small vessels<sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Endothelial infection causes <strong>vasculitis</strong>, <strong>increased vascular permeability</strong>, oedema, <strong>hypovolaemia</strong>, <strong>hypoalbuminaemia</strong>, <strong>hyponatraemia</strong> and multi-organ damage (<strong>brain, lungs, kidneys</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk factors for severe disease:</strong> <strong>older age</strong>, <strong>diabetes</strong>, <strong>alcohol abuse</strong>, <strong>G6PD deficiency</strong>, <strong>delay in doxycycline</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Anaplasmosis and ehrlichiosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><em>A. phagocytophilum</em> lives in a <strong>vacuole of neutrophils</strong>, <em>E. chaffeensis</em> in <strong>monocytes</strong>; the bacterial colonies form <strong>morulae</strong> (\"mulberries\") visible on the smear<sup class=\"cite\">[6,19]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Infection impairs phagocyte function, which explains <strong>leukopenia</strong> and <strong>opportunistic infections</strong> in severe cases<sup class=\"cite\">[19]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3.4", "title": "Tularemia", "html": "<ul>\n<li><em>F. tularensis</em> survives and multiplies inside <strong>macrophages</strong>, escaping the phagosome into the cytoplasm<sup class=\"cite\">[16,23]</sup>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>portal of entry determines the clinical form</strong> (skin, eye, oropharynx, lungs), with spread to <strong>regional lymph nodes</strong> that often <strong>suppurate</strong> and, in severe cases, <strong>bacteraemia</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[16,23]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3.5", "title": "Babesiosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><em>Babesia</em> invades <strong>red blood cells</strong> and multiplies by budding, causing <strong>haemolysis</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>spleen</strong> is essential to clear infected red cells: <strong>asplenia</strong> allows <strong>very high parasitaemia</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Unlike <em>Plasmodium</em>, <em>Babesia</em> has <strong>no liver stage</strong> and produces <strong>no haemozoin pigment</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3.6", "title": "Tick-borne relapsing fever", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Spirochetes multiply in the <strong>blood</strong> to very high numbers. Antibodies clear them, but a minority <strong>change their surface proteins</strong> (variable major proteins) and cause the <strong>next relapse</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n<li>The spirochetes can invade the <strong>central nervous system</strong> and the <strong>placenta</strong> (severe disease in pregnancy, fetal loss)<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3.7", "title": "Tick-borne encephalitis", "html": "<ul>\n<li>The virus replicates first in the <strong>skin</strong> (dendritic cells) and <strong>regional lymph nodes</strong>, causing a <strong>viraemic phase</strong>, then crosses into the <strong>CNS</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li>In the CNS it damages neurons of the <strong>brainstem, cerebellum, basal ganglia, thalamus</strong> and <strong>anterior horn cells of the spinal cord</strong> (flaccid paralysis)<sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Most infections are asymptomatic</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[9]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3.8", "title": "Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever", "html": "<ul>\n<li>The virus infects <strong>macrophages, dendritic cells, hepatocytes and endothelial cells</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[12,13]</sup>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>\"cytokine storm\"</strong>, <strong>liver damage</strong>, <strong>endothelial dysfunction</strong> and <strong>disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)</strong> lead to <strong>bleeding</strong>, <strong>shock</strong> and <strong>multi-organ failure</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[12,13]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "4", "title": "Signs and Symptoms", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "4.1", "title": "Lyme borreliosis", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Timing after bite</th>\n<th>Manifestations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Early localised</strong></td>\n<td><strong>3–30 days</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Erythema migrans</strong>: expanding red macule or patch <strong>≥5 cm</strong>, often with <strong>central clearing</strong> (\"bull's eye\"), usually <strong>not painful or itchy</strong>; may be accompanied by fever, myalgia, headache<sup class=\"cite\">[1,3]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Early disseminated</strong></td>\n<td>Weeks to months</td>\n<td><strong>Multiple EM</strong>; <strong>neuroborreliosis</strong> (lymphocytic meningitis, <strong>cranial neuritis especially facial palsy</strong>, often <strong>bilateral</strong>; <strong>painful radiculitis</strong>); <strong>Lyme carditis</strong> (<strong>AV block</strong>, which can fluctuate quickly); <strong>borrelial lymphocytoma</strong> (bluish-red nodule of the <strong>earlobe</strong> in children, <strong>nipple</strong> in adults)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2,3]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Late</strong></td>\n<td>Months to years</td>\n<td><strong>Lyme arthritis</strong> (recurrent, <strong>mono- or oligoarthritis of large joints</strong>, especially the <strong>knee</strong>, with large effusion and little pain; more common in the <strong>USA</strong>); <strong>ACA</strong> (bluish-red, then <strong>atrophic skin</strong> of the <strong>extensor surfaces of the limbs</strong>, older women, Europe, <em>B. afzelii</em>); late neuroborreliosis (encephalomyelitis, peripheral neuropathy)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2,3]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>European neuroborreliosis:</strong> the classic presentation is <strong>Bannwarth syndrome</strong> = <strong>painful meningoradiculitis</strong> (severe radicular pain, often worse at night) with <strong>cranial nerve palsy</strong> and lymphocytic CSF, often <strong>without headache</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[3,4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Many patients do not remember the tick bite</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-treatment Lyme disease syndrome (PTLDS):</strong> fatigue, pain or cognitive complaints lasting more than 6 months after appropriate treatment; it is <strong>not</strong> due to persistent infection<sup class=\"cite\">[1,14,15]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Spotted fever rickettsioses", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Disease</th>\n<th>Clinical features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mediterranean spotted fever</strong> (<em>R. conorii</em>)</td>\n<td>Incubation about <strong>1 week</strong>. <strong>High fever</strong>, headache, myalgia; <strong>tache noire</strong> (painless black <strong>eschar</strong> with red halo at the bite site, usually <strong>single</strong>); <strong>maculopapular rash</strong> from the 3rd–5th day, <strong>involving palms and soles</strong>. <strong>Severe (\"malignant\") forms</strong> in a minority: neurological signs, renal failure, respiratory distress, haemorrhage<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>RMSF</strong> (<em>R. rickettsii</em>)</td>\n<td>Fever, severe <strong>headache</strong>; <strong>rash from day 2–5</strong> starting at <strong>wrists and ankles</strong>, spreading <strong>centripetally</strong>, including <strong>palms and soles</strong>, becoming <strong>petechial</strong>; eschar <strong>usually absent</strong>. <strong>Often fatal if doxycycline is delayed</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>African tick bite fever</strong> (<em>R. africae</em>)</td>\n<td><strong>Mild</strong>; often <strong>multiple eschars</strong>, <strong>regional lymphadenopathy</strong>, sparse or <strong>vesicular rash</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><em>R. slovaca</em></strong> (TIBOLA/SENLAT)</td>\n<td><strong>Eschar on the scalp</strong> with <strong>painful cervical lymphadenopathy</strong>; may leave <strong>local alopecia</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Typical laboratory findings:</strong> <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong>, <strong>raised transaminases</strong>, <strong>hyponatraemia</strong>, normal or low white cells<sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.3", "title": "Anaplasmosis and ehrlichiosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Incubation 1–2 weeks. <strong>Non-specific febrile illness</strong>: fever, headache, myalgia, malaise<sup class=\"cite\">[6,19]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rash is rare in anaplasmosis</strong>; it is <strong>more frequent in ehrlichiosis</strong>, especially in children<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Laboratory triad:</strong> <strong>leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, raised transaminases</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6,19]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Severe forms</strong> (older or immunocompromised patients): respiratory failure, renal failure, <strong>haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis</strong>, opportunistic infections; <strong>ehrlichiosis is more severe</strong> than anaplasmosis<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.4", "title": "Tularemia", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Incubation usually <strong>3–5 days</strong> (1–14). <strong>Abrupt fever</strong>, chills, headache<sup class=\"cite\">[16,23]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ulceroglandular</strong> (most common)</td>\n<td><strong>Skin ulcer</strong> at the bite or contact site + <strong>tender regional lymphadenopathy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Glandular</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Lymphadenopathy without ulcer</strong> (the most frequent form in some European areas)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Oculoglandular</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Conjunctivitis</strong> + preauricular/cervical nodes (touching the eye with contaminated fingers)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Oropharyngeal</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> + <strong>cervical lymphadenopathy</strong> after <strong>contaminated water or food</strong>; does <strong>not</strong> respond to β-lactams</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pneumonic</strong></td>\n<td>Inhalation or haematogenous spread; atypical pneumonia, hilar nodes, pleural effusion; the most severe form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Typhoidal</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Fever and sepsis without a localising sign</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lymph nodes often suppurate</strong> and can drain, even during correct therapy<sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.5", "title": "Babesiosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Incubation <strong>1–4 weeks</strong> after a bite (longer after transfusion)<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Many infections are <strong>asymptomatic</strong>. Symptomatic disease is <strong>malaria-like</strong>: <strong>fever</strong>, chills, sweats, fatigue, <strong>haemolytic anaemia</strong>, <strong>jaundice</strong>, <strong>dark urine</strong>, splenomegaly<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Severe disease</strong> (asplenia, immunosuppression, older age): <strong>ARDS</strong>, <strong>DIC</strong>, <strong>renal failure</strong>, <strong>congestive heart failure</strong>, <strong>splenic rupture</strong>, death<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Laboratory:</strong> haemolytic anaemia (low haptoglobin, high LDH, high indirect bilirubin, reticulocytosis), <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong>, raised transaminases<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.6", "title": "Tick-borne relapsing fever", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Incubation about <strong>1 week</strong>. <strong>Recurrent febrile episodes</strong> lasting <strong>about 3 days</strong>, separated by <strong>afebrile intervals of about 1 week</strong>; tick-borne forms give <strong>several relapses</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Fever ends with a <strong>\"crisis\"</strong> (chills, hypotension). Other features: headache, myalgia, <strong>hepatosplenomegaly</strong>, <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong>, raised transaminases; <strong>neurological involvement</strong> (meningitis, <strong>facial palsy</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong><em>B. miyamotoi</em> disease:</strong> flu-like fever <strong>without EM</strong>, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia; <strong>meningoencephalitis</strong> in immunocompromised patients<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.7", "title": "Tick-borne encephalitis", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Incubation <strong>about 1 week</strong> (range 4–28 days)<sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Biphasic course</strong> (typical of the <strong>European subtype</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>:\n  1. <strong>Viraemic phase</strong> (2–7 days): <strong>non-specific flu-like illness</strong> (fever, fatigue, headache, myalgia), often with <strong>leukopenia and thrombocytopenia</strong>.\n  2. <strong>Asymptomatic interval</strong> of about <strong>1 week</strong>.\n  3. <strong>Neurological phase:</strong> <strong>meningitis</strong> (about half of cases; more common in younger patients), <strong>meningoencephalitis</strong> (consciousness changes, ataxia, tremor, cranial nerve palsies), <strong>meningoencephalomyelitis</strong> with <strong>flaccid paralysis</strong>, typically of the <strong>shoulder girdle and arms</strong>, resembling poliomyelitis.</li>\n<li><strong>Severity increases with age</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sequelae</strong> (post-encephalitic syndrome: cognitive problems, headache, fatigue, ataxia, paresis) occur in <strong>up to a third or more</strong> of patients with neurological disease<sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Case fatality:</strong> <strong>under 2%</strong> for the European subtype, <strong>higher (up to 20% or more)</strong> for the Far Eastern subtype; the Siberian subtype can cause <strong>chronic progressive</strong> disease<sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.8", "title": "Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Incubation <strong>1–3 days after a tick bite</strong> (maximum 9) and <strong>5–6 days after contact with blood or tissues</strong> (maximum 13)<sup class=\"cite\">[25]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Phases</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[12,13]</sup>:\n  1. <strong>Pre-haemorrhagic phase</strong> (about 3 days): <strong>sudden high fever</strong>, headache, <strong>myalgia</strong>, dizziness, photophobia, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, sometimes mood changes and confusion.\n  2. <strong>Haemorrhagic phase</strong> (from day 3–5): <strong>petechiae, large ecchymoses</strong>, <strong>epistaxis</strong>, gum bleeding, <strong>haematemesis</strong>, <strong>melaena</strong>, haematuria; <strong>hepatomegaly</strong>; shock and multi-organ failure. Death usually occurs in the <strong>second week</strong>.\n  3. <strong>Convalescence</strong>: slow, with weakness, sometimes hair loss.</li>\n<li><strong>Laboratory:</strong> <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong>, leukopenia, <strong>very high AST/ALT, LDH and CK</strong>, prolonged PT/aPTT, low fibrinogen (<strong>DIC</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[12,13]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.9", "title": "Clinical clues across tick-borne infections", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Clue</th>\n<th>Think of</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Expanding erythema</strong> ≥5 cm</td>\n<td>Lyme borreliosis (EM)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Eschar (tache noire)</strong> + rash on palms and soles</td>\n<td>Mediterranean spotted fever</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Petechial rash</strong> starting at wrists and ankles</td>\n<td>RMSF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fever + leukopenia + thrombocytopenia + raised transaminases</strong>, no rash</td>\n<td>Anaplasmosis/ehrlichiosis; also early TBE, <em>B. miyamotoi</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fever + haemolysis</strong>, asplenic patient</td>\n<td>Babesiosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ulcer + suppurating lymph node</strong>, hunter or hare contact</td>\n<td>Tularemia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Recurring fever every week</strong>, travel to Africa or Iberia</td>\n<td>Tick-borne relapsing fever</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Biphasic illness</strong>, then meningoencephalitis or flaccid arm paresis</td>\n<td>TBE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fever + bleeding</strong> after tick bite or slaughtering livestock (Balkans, Turkey, Spain)</td>\n<td>CCHF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Facial palsy</strong> (especially bilateral), AV block, knee effusion</td>\n<td>Disseminated Lyme borreliosis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}]}, {"id": "5", "title": "Diagnosis", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "5.1", "title": "When to suspect a tick-borne infection", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Exposure:</strong> outdoor activity in <strong>endemic areas</strong> (woods, meadows, rural areas), <strong>season</strong>, <strong>known tick bite</strong> (often absent), <strong>animal contact</strong> (dogs, hares, livestock), <strong>travel</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,5,6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compatible syndrome:</strong> see table 4.9. Always ask about <strong>splenectomy</strong>, <strong>immunosuppression</strong>, <strong>pregnancy</strong> and <strong>TBE vaccination</strong> status<sup class=\"cite\">[7,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Co-infection</strong> (for example Lyme + anaplasmosis or babesiosis) should be suspected when a patient with Lyme disease has <strong>high fever</strong>, <strong>leukopenia, thrombocytopenia or anaemia</strong>, or <strong>does not improve</strong> on treatment<sup class=\"cite\">[1,7]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.2", "title": "Lyme borreliosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Typical EM: clinical diagnosis, no laboratory test needed</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,3]</sup>. Serology is <strong>often negative</strong> in the first weeks (sensitivity about 30–40% for EM)<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Two-tier serology</strong> for <strong>extracutaneous</strong> manifestations<sup class=\"cite\">[1,5]</sup>:</li>\n<li><strong>Standard two-tier:</strong> screening <strong>immunoassay (EIA/ELISA)</strong>, followed by <strong>immunoblot</strong> (Western blot) if positive or equivocal.</li>\n<li><strong>Modified two-tier</strong> (approved by FDA, endorsed by CDC 2019): <strong>two different immunoassays</strong> in sequence<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>IgM</strong> results are reliable only in the <strong>first 4 weeks</strong> of illness; an isolated positive IgM after that is likely a <strong>false positive</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Antibodies <strong>persist for years</strong> after cure, so serology <strong>cannot be used to judge cure</strong>, and positive results are common in exposed but healthy people<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Neuroborreliosis</strong> (EFNS criteria)<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>:</li>\n<li><strong>Definite:</strong> neurological symptoms + <strong>CSF lymphocytic pleocytosis</strong> + <strong>intrathecal anti-<em>Borrelia</em> antibody production</strong> (raised <strong>CSF/serum antibody index</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>CXCL13</strong> in CSF is a sensitive marker of early neuroborreliosis (supportive, not specific)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Lyme arthritis:</strong> serum <strong>IgG</strong> is almost always <strong>strongly positive</strong>; <strong>PCR of synovial fluid</strong> supports the diagnosis<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Lyme carditis:</strong> <strong>ECG</strong> in patients with cardiac symptoms (palpitations, syncope, dyspnoea, chest pain)<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>ACA:</strong> serology (high IgG) and <strong>skin biopsy</strong> (histology, PCR)<sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Do NOT test</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>: patients with <strong>non-specific symptoms only</strong> (fatigue, myalgia) without exposure; <strong>asymptomatic people after a tick bite</strong>; <strong>the tick itself</strong>. <strong>Do not use</strong> non-validated tests (lymphocyte transformation tests, CD57 counts, urine antigen tests).</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.3", "title": "Spotted fever rickettsioses", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Treat on clinical suspicion; do not wait for confirmation</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Best early test:</strong> <strong>PCR (or culture) of an eschar swab or biopsy</strong>; PCR on blood is less sensitive<sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Serology (indirect immunofluorescence, IFA)</strong> is the reference method, but antibodies appear only <strong>7–10 days</strong> after onset: diagnosis needs <strong>seroconversion or a fourfold rise in IgG</strong> between acute and convalescent samples (2–4 weeks apart)<sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup>.</li>\n<li>The old <strong>Weil-Felix test</strong> is <strong>insensitive and non-specific</strong> and should not be used<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.4", "title": "Anaplasmosis and ehrlichiosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood PCR</strong> (EDTA blood) is the <strong>test of choice</strong> in the first week of illness, before doxycycline<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood smear:</strong> <strong>morulae</strong> in <strong>neutrophils</strong> (<em>Anaplasma</em>) or <strong>monocytes</strong> (<em>Ehrlichia</em>); quick but insensitive<sup class=\"cite\">[6,19]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Serology (IFA):</strong> fourfold IgG rise between paired samples confirms the diagnosis retrospectively<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.5", "title": "Tularemia", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Serology</strong> (microagglutination or ELISA) is the most used test: antibodies appear after <strong>about 2 weeks</strong>; seroconversion or a <strong>fourfold rise</strong> confirms<sup class=\"cite\">[16,23]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>PCR</strong> on ulcer swabs, lymph node aspirates or tissue gives an early diagnosis<sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Culture</strong> needs <strong>cysteine-enriched media</strong> and a <strong>biosafety level 3</strong> laboratory: <strong>warn the laboratory</strong>, because <em>F. tularensis</em> is a major cause of <strong>laboratory-acquired infection</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[16,23]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.6", "title": "Babesiosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Thin and thick Giemsa-stained blood smears</strong> show <strong>intraerythrocytic ring forms</strong>; the <strong>\"Maltese cross\"</strong> (tetrad) is pathognomonic but uncommon<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Differences from <em>P. falciparum</em>:</strong> <strong>no pigment (haemozoin)</strong>, <strong>no schizonts or gametocytes</strong>, <strong>extracellular merozoites</strong>, rings variable in size, <strong>travel history</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood PCR</strong> is <strong>more sensitive</strong> than the smear and confirms the species<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Parasitaemia</strong> (% infected red cells) must be measured: it guides treatment and follow-up<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.7", "title": "Tick-borne relapsing fever", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Spirochetes on a Giemsa or Wright-stained blood smear</strong> (thin or thick) collected <strong>during a febrile episode</strong> (they are scarce between attacks)<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood PCR</strong> is more sensitive and identifies the species<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Serology</strong> is not standardised and <strong>cross-reacts with Lyme serology</strong>; <em>B. miyamotoi</em> is diagnosed by <strong>PCR</strong> or anti-<strong>GlpQ</strong> antibodies (a protein absent in Lyme <em>Borrelia</em>)<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.8", "title": "Tick-borne encephalitis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>CSF:</strong> <strong>lymphocytic pleocytosis</strong> (moderate), slightly raised protein, normal glucose<sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Serology:</strong> <strong>specific IgM and IgG in serum</strong> (ELISA), usually <strong>already positive when neurological symptoms start</strong>; <strong>intrathecal antibody production</strong> in CSF<sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pitfalls</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[10]</sup>:</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-reactivity</strong> with other <strong>flaviviruses</strong> (dengue, West Nile, yellow fever, Japanese encephalitis) and with <strong>TBE or yellow fever vaccination</strong>: confirm with <strong>neutralisation tests</strong> or CSF antibody index.</li>\n<li><strong>RT-PCR</strong> is useful only in the <strong>first (viraemic) phase</strong>; it is usually <strong>negative</strong> in the neurological phase.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI</strong> may show T2 hyperintensities in the <strong>thalamus, basal ganglia, brainstem, cerebellum</strong> or spinal anterior horns<sup class=\"cite\">[10]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.9", "title": "Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Suspect CCHF in a patient with <strong>fever and bleeding</strong> within <strong>14 days</strong> of a <strong>tick bite</strong> or <strong>contact with livestock or a CCHF patient</strong> in an endemic area<sup class=\"cite\">[25]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Immediately isolate</strong> the patient and <strong>notify</strong> public health authorities<sup class=\"cite\">[25]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>RT-PCR</strong> on blood is the test of choice in the first days; <strong>IgM and IgG</strong> (ELISA) appear from around <strong>day 5–7</strong>; samples are handled in <strong>high-containment laboratories</strong> (BSL-4 for culture)<sup class=\"cite\">[12,13,25]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.10", "title": "Summary of preferred tests", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Disease</th>\n<th>Early (first week)</th>\n<th>Later / confirmation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lyme (EM)</td>\n<td><strong>Clinical</strong></td>\n<td>Not needed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lyme (disseminated/late)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>Two-tier serology</strong>; <strong>CSF antibody index</strong> (neuro); synovial PCR (arthritis)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spotted fevers</td>\n<td><strong>Eschar PCR</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Paired IFA serology</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anaplasmosis/ehrlichiosis</td>\n<td><strong>Blood PCR</strong>, morulae on smear</td>\n<td>Paired IFA serology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tularemia</td>\n<td><strong>PCR</strong> on ulcer/node</td>\n<td><strong>Serology</strong> (from week 2); culture in BSL-3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Babesiosis</td>\n<td><strong>Smear + PCR</strong>, parasitaemia</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TBRF</td>\n<td><strong>Smear during fever</strong>, PCR</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TBE</td>\n<td>RT-PCR (viraemic phase only)</td>\n<td><strong>Serum and CSF IgM/IgG</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CCHF</td>\n<td><strong>RT-PCR</strong></td>\n<td>IgM/IgG</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}]}, {"id": "6", "title": "Therapy", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "6.1", "title": "Lyme borreliosis (IDSA/AAN/ACR 2020; European guidelines)", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Manifestation</th>\n<th>First-line</th>\n<th>Alternatives</th>\n<th>Duration (total)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Erythema migrans</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Doxycycline</strong> (oral)</td>\n<td><strong>Amoxicillin</strong> or <strong>cefuroxime axetil</strong> (oral); macrolide (azithromycin) only if the others cannot be used</td>\n<td><strong>10 days</strong> doxycycline; <strong>14 days</strong> amoxicillin or cefuroxime<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Facial palsy, meningitis, radiculoneuritis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Oral doxycycline</strong> or <strong>IV ceftriaxone</strong> (equally effective)</td>\n<td>IV penicillin G, cefotaxime</td>\n<td><strong>14–21 days</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lyme carditis</strong></td>\n<td>Oral (as EM) or <strong>IV ceftriaxone</strong> for hospitalised patients, then oral</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>14–21 days</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lyme arthritis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Oral</strong> doxycycline, amoxicillin or cefuroxime</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>28 days</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Persistent arthritis after one course</strong></td>\n<td>Second <strong>oral</strong> course <strong>or</strong> <strong>IV ceftriaxone</strong> (2–4 weeks)</td>\n<td>If still active: <strong>NSAIDs, DMARDs, intra-articular steroids, arthroscopic synovectomy</strong> (refractory arthritis is immune-mediated)</td>\n<td>[1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ACA</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Oral doxycycline</strong></td>\n<td>Amoxicillin, cefuroxime</td>\n<td><strong>21–28 days</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,3]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Borrelial lymphocytoma</strong></td>\n<td>As EM</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>14 days</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Doxycycline in children:</strong> short courses (up to 21 days) are <strong>acceptable at any age</strong>; the risk of <strong>tooth staining</strong> is negligible<sup class=\"cite\">[1,6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Lyme carditis</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>: <strong>hospitalise</strong> patients with <strong>PR interval ≥300 ms</strong>, <strong>second- or third-degree AV block</strong>, syncope or dyspnoea; use <strong>temporary pacing</strong> if needed. <strong>Do not implant a permanent pacemaker</strong> early, because the <strong>block usually resolves</strong> with antibiotics.</li>\n<li><strong>Late neuroborreliosis</strong> (encephalomyelitis): IV ceftriaxone, usually longer courses<sup class=\"cite\">[1,4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> <strong>amoxicillin</strong> or <strong>cefuroxime</strong>; <strong>doxycycline is generally avoided</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction</strong> (fever, worsening rash in the first 24 h) can occur in a minority of EM patients; it is <strong>not an allergy</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-treatment Lyme disease syndrome:</strong> <strong>prolonged or repeated antibiotic courses are NOT recommended</strong>; randomised trials showed <strong>no benefit</strong> and real harm (IV catheter infections, <em>C. difficile</em>, allergy)<sup class=\"cite\">[1,14,15]</sup>. Manage symptoms and rule out other diagnoses.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.2", "title": "Spotted fever rickettsioses", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Doxycycline is the drug of choice for all ages</strong>, including <strong>children under 8</strong>; it should be <strong>started empirically</strong> as soon as the disease is suspected<sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Treatment duration:</strong> until at least <strong>3 days after defervescence</strong> (usually 5–7 days in total)<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Alternatives</strong> (MSF): <strong>chloramphenicol</strong> (severe cases, if doxycycline is not available); <strong>macrolides</strong> (azithromycin, clarithromycin) for <strong>mild MSF</strong> in <strong>pregnancy</strong> or children<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fluoroquinolones should be avoided</strong> in MSF: clinical data associated them with <strong>more severe disease</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5,26]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>β-lactams, aminoglycosides and co-trimoxazole are inactive</strong> against rickettsiae<sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.3", "title": "Anaplasmosis and ehrlichiosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Doxycycline</strong> for all ages, usually <strong>10 days</strong> (or until 3 days after defervescence); 10–14 days if Lyme co-infection must be treated too<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Fever should <strong>resolve within 24–48 h</strong>: if not, reconsider the diagnosis (including <strong>co-infection with <em>Babesia</em></strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rifampicin</strong> is an alternative for <strong>mild anaplasmosis</strong> in <strong>pregnancy</strong> or doxycycline allergy<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.4", "title": "Tularemia", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Preferred</th>\n<th>Comments</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mild to moderate</strong> (especially type B, Europe)</td>\n<td><strong>Ciprofloxacin</strong> (oral)</td>\n<td>Good efficacy and bactericidal<sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>Doxycycline</strong></td>\n<td>Bacteriostatic: <strong>higher relapse rate</strong>; longer course needed<sup class=\"cite\">[16,23]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Severe</strong> (pneumonic, typhoidal, sepsis)</td>\n<td><strong>Gentamicin</strong> (or <strong>streptomycin</strong>) IV</td>\n<td>Can be combined with or followed by ciprofloxacin; meningitis needs additional agents<sup class=\"cite\">[16,23]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>β-lactams and macrolides are ineffective</strong> (<em>F. tularensis</em> produces β-lactamase; type B strains are resistant to erythromycin in some regions)<sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Suppurated lymph nodes</strong> may require <strong>drainage or excision</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.5", "title": "Babesiosis (IDSA 2020)", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Treat all symptomatic patients</strong> with active infection (positive smear or PCR)<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Asymptomatic</strong> patients: treat only if parasites persist for <strong>more than 3 months</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>First-line, mild and severe disease:</strong> <strong>atovaquone + azithromycin</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Alternative:</strong> <strong>clindamycin + quinine</strong> (more side effects)<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Duration:</strong> <strong>7–10 days</strong> for most patients; in <strong>highly immunocompromised</strong> patients <strong>at least 6 weeks</strong>, including <strong>2 weeks after the smear becomes negative</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Red blood cell exchange transfusion</strong> for <strong>high parasitaemia (&gt;10%)</strong> and/or <strong>severe haemolysis</strong>, or <strong>organ failure</strong> (lung, liver, kidney)<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.6", "title": "Tick-borne relapsing fever", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Doxycycline</strong> or a <strong>macrolide</strong> (oral), or a <strong>β-lactam</strong> (penicillin, ceftriaxone); <strong>IV ceftriaxone</strong> for <strong>CNS involvement</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction</strong> is <strong>frequent</strong> (rigors, high fever, hypotension in the first hours): <strong>monitor</strong> the patient after the first dose<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong><em>B. miyamotoi</em>:</strong> treated like Lyme disease (doxycycline; ceftriaxone for meningoencephalitis)<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.7", "title": "Tick-borne encephalitis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>No antiviral therapy</strong> is available: treatment is <strong>supportive</strong> (analgesia, antipyretics, fluids, management of <strong>intracranial hypertension</strong> and <strong>seizures</strong>, ventilatory support, rehabilitation)<sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Corticosteroids</strong> and <strong>immunoglobulins</strong> have <strong>no proven benefit</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[10]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.8", "title": "Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Supportive care</strong> is the basis: fluid and electrolyte balance, <strong>platelets, fresh frozen plasma</strong> and red cells as needed, oxygen, organ support<sup class=\"cite\">[12,13,25]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ribavirin</strong> has been widely used, but a <strong>Cochrane review</strong> found <strong>very low-certainty evidence</strong> and <strong>could not show a benefit</strong> on mortality<sup class=\"cite\">[27]</sup>. <strong>Favipiravir</strong> is under study<sup class=\"cite\">[13]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Infection control:</strong> single room, <strong>standard + contact + droplet precautions</strong>; <strong>airborne precautions</strong> (FFP2/FFP3) for <strong>aerosol-generating procedures</strong>; safe handling of blood and waste<sup class=\"cite\">[25]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.9", "title": "Summary: which drug for which tick-borne infection", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Infection</th>\n<th>First-line drug class</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lyme borreliosis</td>\n<td><strong>Doxycycline</strong> / amoxicillin / cefuroxime; <strong>ceftriaxone</strong> for severe neuro or cardiac disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rickettsioses</td>\n<td><strong>Doxycycline</strong> (all ages)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anaplasmosis/ehrlichiosis</td>\n<td><strong>Doxycycline</strong> (all ages)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tularemia</td>\n<td><strong>Ciprofloxacin</strong> (mild) / <strong>gentamicin</strong> (severe)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Babesiosis</td>\n<td><strong>Atovaquone + azithromycin</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relapsing fever</td>\n<td>Doxycycline / penicillin / ceftriaxone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TBE</td>\n<td><strong>Supportive only</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CCHF</td>\n<td><strong>Supportive</strong>; ribavirin of uncertain benefit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}]}, {"id": "7", "title": "Follow-up", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "7.1", "title": "Lyme borreliosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>EM:</strong> the rash fades within <strong>days to weeks</strong>; mild symptoms (fatigue, arthralgia) can last for weeks and <strong>do not need more antibiotics</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not repeat serology</strong> to check cure: antibodies, including IgM, can persist for months or years<sup class=\"cite\">[1,2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Neuroborreliosis:</strong> clinical improvement is gradual; <strong>facial palsy recovers</strong> in most patients over weeks to months; CSF pleocytosis decreases slowly, and a repeat lumbar puncture is not needed if the patient improves<sup class=\"cite\">[1,4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Carditis:</strong> <strong>ECG monitoring</strong> until the AV block resolves<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reinfection</strong> is possible: a <strong>new EM</strong> after a new bite is a <strong>new infection</strong>, not a relapse<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "7.2", "title": "Rickettsioses, anaplasmosis, ehrlichiosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Expect <strong>defervescence within 24–72 hours</strong> of doxycycline; <strong>no fever response suggests another diagnosis</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Check <strong>platelets, transaminases and sodium</strong> until normal<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "7.3", "title": "Tularemia", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Fever falls within a few days, but <strong>lymphadenopathy can persist or suppurate</strong> for weeks; this is not necessarily treatment failure<sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Relapses</strong> occur especially after <strong>doxycycline</strong> or <strong>short courses</strong>; treat again with ciprofloxacin or an aminoglycoside<sup class=\"cite\">[16,23]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "7.4", "title": "Babesiosis", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Monitor <strong>parasitaemia</strong> (smear) <strong>daily</strong> in hospitalised patients until it falls below about 4–5%, and <strong>haemoglobin</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Follow immunocompromised patients with <strong>smears (and PCR)</strong> for relapse; PCR can remain positive for weeks after cure in healthy patients<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Patients with babesiosis are <strong>excluded from blood donation</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "7.5", "title": "Tick-borne encephalitis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Neurological and neuropsychological follow-up</strong> and <strong>rehabilitation</strong>; recovery may take <strong>months</strong>, and some deficits are permanent<sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li>TBE gives <strong>long-lasting immunity</strong> against reinfection<sup class=\"cite\">[9]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "7.6", "title": "Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Contacts</strong> (household members, healthcare workers) are monitored for <strong>fever</strong> for <strong>14 days</strong> after the last exposure<sup class=\"cite\">[25]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Convalescence is long; survivors have <strong>long-lasting antibodies</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "8", "title": "Vaccines and Prophylaxis", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "8.1", "title": "Preventing tick bites", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Personal measures</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,6]</sup>:</li>\n<li>Walk in the <strong>middle of trails</strong>; avoid tall grass and leaf litter.</li>\n<li><strong>Long trousers tucked into socks</strong>, <strong>light-coloured clothes</strong> (ticks are easier to see), closed shoes.</li>\n<li><strong>Repellents on skin:</strong> <strong>DEET</strong>, <strong>icaridin (picaridin)</strong>, <strong>IR3535</strong>, oil of lemon eucalyptus.</li>\n<li><strong>Permethrin-treated clothing</strong> (not on skin).</li>\n<li><strong>Daily full-body tick check</strong> (including scalp, ears, armpits, groin, back of knees; children's heads); shower soon after outdoor activity.</li>\n<li>Control ticks on <strong>dogs and pets</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Tick removal</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,6]</sup>:</li>\n<li>Use <strong>fine-tipped tweezers</strong> (or a tick hook), grasp the tick <strong>as close to the skin as possible</strong>, and <strong>pull upwards with steady, even pressure</strong>, without twisting or crushing the tick.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not</strong> use petroleum jelly, alcohol, nail polish, heat or oil: they delay removal and may increase transmission.</li>\n<li><strong>Disinfect</strong> the site and <strong>wash hands</strong>; note the <strong>date</strong> and watch the site for <strong>30 days</strong> (EM) and the patient for fever.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not crush ticks with bare hands</strong> (CCHF).</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "8.2", "title": "Lyme borreliosis: post-bite prophylaxis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>IDSA/AAN/ACR 2020:</strong> a <strong>single dose of oral doxycycline</strong> within <strong>72 hours of removal</strong> is recommended only for a <strong>high-risk bite</strong> = <strong>all three</strong> of<sup class=\"cite\">[1,11]</sup>:\n  1. Tick identified as an <strong><em>Ixodes</em></strong> species.\n  2. Bite in a <strong>highly endemic area</strong>.\n  3. Tick <strong>attached for ≥36 hours</strong> (or engorged).</li>\n<li>In the landmark trial, single-dose doxycycline reduced EM after <em>I. scapularis</em> bites in the USA (from 3.2% to 0.4%)<sup class=\"cite\">[11]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Europe:</strong> most recommendations <strong>do not advise routine prophylaxis</strong>; the usual approach is to <strong>observe the bite site for 30 days</strong> and treat EM if it appears<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Testing the tick</strong> and <strong>post-bite serology</strong> are <strong>not recommended</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "8.3", "title": "Lyme borreliosis vaccine", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>No vaccine is currently licensed</strong> for humans; a previous <strong>OspA vaccine</strong> (LYMErix) was withdrawn from the US market in <strong>2002</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li>A new <strong>hexavalent OspA vaccine</strong> (<strong>PF-07307405</strong>, formerly <strong>VLA15</strong>; Pfizer/Valneva) was tested in the phase 3 <strong>VALOR</strong> trial (9,437 participants aged ≥5 years in the USA, Canada and Europe; 4 doses). In <strong>March 2026</strong> the companies reported <strong>73.2% efficacy</strong> (95% CI 15.8–93.5) after the fourth dose; because few Lyme cases occurred, the lower bound of the confidence interval <strong>missed</strong> the pre-set success criterion. <strong>Regulatory submissions are planned</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[21]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "8.4", "title": "TBE vaccine", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Inactivated whole-virus vaccines</strong> grown in chick embryo cells (<strong>FSME-IMMUN</strong> and <strong>Encepur</strong> in Europe); <strong>highly effective</strong> (field effectiveness about <strong>95% or more</strong> in Austria) and <strong>cross-protective</strong> against all three subtypes<sup class=\"cite\">[8,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Schedule:</strong> <strong>primary series of 3 doses</strong> (second dose 1–3 months after the first, third dose 5–12 months later; <strong>rapid schedules</strong> exist for travellers), then <strong>first booster after 3 years</strong> and further boosters every <strong>3–5 years</strong> (age-dependent)<sup class=\"cite\">[8,10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>WHO</strong> recommends vaccination of <strong>all age groups</strong> in highly endemic areas (incidence ≥5 cases per 100,000 per year), and of <strong>at-risk groups</strong> where incidence is lower<sup class=\"cite\">[8]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Italy:</strong> the <strong>National Vaccine Prevention Plan (PNPV 2023–2025)</strong> recommends TBE vaccination for people <strong>at risk</strong> because of <strong>residence, work or leisure activities</strong> in endemic areas (for example forestry workers, farmers, hikers)<sup class=\"cite\">[18]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pasteurisation</strong> of milk prevents alimentary transmission<sup class=\"cite\">[9]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "8.5", "title": "Other tick-borne infections", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>No licensed vaccines</strong> for rickettsioses, anaplasmosis, ehrlichiosis, babesiosis, relapsing fever or tularemia (a live attenuated tularemia vaccine is used only in a few settings, e.g. laboratory workers)<sup class=\"cite\">[16,23]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>CCHF:</strong> <strong>no internationally licensed vaccine</strong> (an inactivated vaccine has been used locally in Bulgaria)<sup class=\"cite\">[12,13]</sup>. Prevention relies on <strong>tick-bite avoidance</strong>, <strong>protective clothing for livestock workers</strong>, and <strong>strict infection control</strong> in hospitals<sup class=\"cite\">[25]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Babesiosis:</strong> <strong>blood donor screening</strong> (in US endemic areas) prevents transfusion-transmitted infection; <strong>asplenic</strong> patients should be especially careful in endemic areas<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-exposure prophylaxis</strong> is <strong>not recommended</strong> after tick bites for rickettsioses, anaplasmosis, ehrlichiosis or TBE<sup class=\"cite\">[1,6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}], "open": ["Compare the main tick-borne infections seen in Italy (Lyme borreliosis, Mediterranean spotted fever and tick-borne encephalitis) in terms of causative agent, typical clinical presentation and treatment.", "Describe the clinical stages of Lyme borreliosis and explain how the infecting genospecies (<em>B. afzelii</em>, <em>B. garinii</em>, <em>B. burgdorferi</em> sensu stricto) influences the clinical picture in Europe and North America.", "Explain the principles and limitations of two-tier Lyme serology. In which situations should serology not be requested?", "How is Lyme neuroborreliosis diagnosed and treated? Describe Bannwarth syndrome.", "Describe the clinical presentation, diagnosis and treatment of Mediterranean spotted fever, and explain why treatment should never be delayed until serology is available.", "Compare human granulocytic anaplasmosis and human monocytic ehrlichiosis (agent, vector, target cells, laboratory findings, treatment).", "List the clinical forms of tularemia and explain how the route of infection determines the presentation. Which antibiotics are used, and which are ineffective?", "How is babesiosis distinguished from malaria on a blood smear? Who is at risk of severe babesiosis and how is it treated?", "Describe the natural history of tick-borne encephalitis, its diagnosis and the main features of TBE vaccination.", "Describe the transmission routes, clinical phases and infection control measures for Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever."], "refs": "<ol>\n<li>Lantos PM, Rumbaugh J, Bockenstedt LK, et al. Clinical practice guidelines by the Infectious Diseases Society of America (IDSA), American Academy of Neurology (AAN), and American College of Rheumatology (ACR): 2020 guidelines for the prevention, diagnosis and treatment of Lyme disease. <em>Clin Infect Dis</em>. 2021;72(1):e1–e48. https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciaa1215</li>\n<li>Stanek G, Wormser GP, Gray J, Strle F. Lyme borreliosis. <em>Lancet</em>. 2012;379(9814):461–473. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(11)60103-7</li>\n<li>Stanek G, Fingerle V, Hunfeld KP, et al. Lyme borreliosis: clinical case definitions for diagnosis and management in Europe. <em>Clin Microbiol Infect</em>. 2011;17(1):69–79. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-0691.2010.03175.x</li>\n<li>Mygland Å, Ljøstad U, Fingerle V, et al. EFNS guidelines on the diagnosis and management of European Lyme neuroborreliosis. <em>Eur J Neurol</em>. 2010;17(1):8–16. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-1331.2009.02862.x</li>\n<li>Parola P, Paddock CD, Socolovschi C, et al. Update on tick-borne rickettsioses around the world: a geographic approach. <em>Clin Microbiol Rev</em>. 2013;26(4):657–702. https://doi.org/10.1128/CMR.00032-13</li>\n<li>Biggs HM, Behravesh CB, Bradley KK, et al. Diagnosis and management of tickborne rickettsial diseases: Rocky Mountain spotted fever and other spotted fever group rickettsioses, ehrlichioses, and anaplasmosis — United States. <em>MMWR Recomm Rep</em>. 2016;65(2):1–44. https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.rr6502a1</li>\n<li>Krause PJ, Auwaerter PG, Bannuru RR, et al. Clinical practice guidelines by the Infectious Diseases Society of America (IDSA): 2020 guideline on diagnosis and management of babesiosis. <em>Clin Infect Dis</em>. 2021;72(2):e49–e64. https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciaa1216</li>\n<li>World Health Organization. Vaccines against tick-borne encephalitis: WHO position paper. <em>Wkly Epidemiol Rec</em>. 2011;86(24):241–256. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21661276/</li>\n<li>Lindquist L, Vapalahti O. Tick-borne encephalitis. <em>Lancet</em>. 2008;371(9627):1861–1871. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(08)60800-4</li>\n<li>Taba P, Schmutzhard E, Forsberg P, et al. EAN consensus review on prevention, diagnosis and management of tick-borne encephalitis. <em>Eur J Neurol</em>. 2017;24(10):1214–e61. https://doi.org/10.1111/ene.13356</li>\n<li>Nadelman RB, Nowakowski J, Fish D, et al. Prophylaxis with single-dose doxycycline for the prevention of Lyme disease after an <em>Ixodes scapularis</em> tick bite. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2001;345(2):79–84. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM200107123450201</li>\n<li>Bente DA, Forrester NL, Watts DM, et al. Crimean-Congo hemorrhagic fever: history, epidemiology, pathogenesis, clinical syndrome and genetic diversity. <em>Antiviral Res</em>. 2013;100(1):159–189. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.antiviral.2013.07.006</li>\n<li>Hawman DW, Feldmann H. Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever virus. <em>Nat Rev Microbiol</em>. 2023;21(7):463–477. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41579-023-00871-9</li>\n<li>Klempner MS, Hu LT, Evans J, et al. Two controlled trials of antibiotic treatment in patients with persistent symptoms and a history of Lyme disease. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2001;345(2):85–92. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM200107123450202</li>\n<li>Berende A, ter Hofstede HJM, Vos FJ, et al. Randomized trial of longer-term therapy for symptoms attributed to Lyme disease. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2016;374(13):1209–1220. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1505425</li>\n<li>Maurin M, Gyuranecz M. Tularaemia: clinical aspects in Europe. <em>Lancet Infect Dis</em>. 2016;16(1):113–124. https://doi.org/10.1016/S1473-3099(15)00355-2</li>\n<li>Talagrand-Reboul E, Boyer PH, Bergström S, et al. Relapsing fevers: neglected tick-borne diseases. <em>Front Cell Infect Microbiol</em>. 2018;8:98. https://doi.org/10.3389/fcimb.2018.00098</li>\n<li>Ministero della Salute. Piano Nazionale di Prevenzione Vaccinale (PNPV) 2023–2025. <em>Gazzetta Ufficiale</em>, 21 August 2023. Summary: https://www.epicentro.iss.it/vaccini/piano-nazionale-vaccini-2023-2025</li>\n<li>Bakken JS, Dumler JS. Human granulocytic anaplasmosis. <em>Infect Dis Clin North Am</em>. 2015;29(2):341–355. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.idc.2015.02.007</li>\n<li>European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control. Tick-borne encephalitis. Annual Epidemiological Report for 2022. Stockholm: ECDC; 2024. https://www.ecdc.europa.eu/en/publications-data/tick-borne-encephalitis-annual-epidemiological-report-2022</li>\n<li>Pfizer, Valneva. Pfizer and Valneva announce Lyme disease vaccine candidate demonstrates strong efficacy in phase 3 VALOR trial [press release]. 23 March 2026. https://valneva.com/press-release/pfizer-and-valneva-announce-lyme-disease-vaccine-candidate-demonstrates-strong-efficacy-in-phase-3-valor-trial/</li>\n<li>Kugeler KJ, Schwartz AM, Delorey MJ, Mead PS, Hinckley AF. Estimating the frequency of Lyme disease diagnoses, United States, 2010–2018. <em>Emerg Infect Dis</em>. 2021;27(2):616–619. https://doi.org/10.3201/eid2702.202731</li>\n<li>World Health Organization. WHO guidelines on tularaemia. Geneva: WHO; 2007. https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241547376</li>\n<li>Negredo A, de la Calle-Prieto F, Palencia-Herrejón E, et al. Autochthonous Crimean-Congo hemorrhagic fever in Spain. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2017;377(2):154–161. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1615162</li>\n<li>World Health Organization. Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever: fact sheet. https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/crimean-congo-haemorrhagic-fever (accessed November 2026)</li>\n<li>Botelho-Nevers E, Edouard S, Leroy Q, Raoult D. Deleterious effect of ciprofloxacin on <em>Rickettsia conorii</em>-infected cells is linked to toxin-antitoxin module up-regulation. <em>J Antimicrob Chemother</em>. 2012;67(7):1677–1682. https://doi.org/10.1093/jac/dks089</li>\n<li>Johnson S, Henschke N, Maayan N, et al. Ribavirin for treating Crimean Congo haemorrhagic fever. <em>Cochrane Database Syst Rev</em>. 2018;6(6):CD012713. https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD012713.pub2</li>\n<li>Bennett JE, Dolin R, Blaser MJ, eds. <em>Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases</em>. 9th ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2020 (chapters on ticks and tick-borne diseases).</li>\n</ol>"};
